              "response": "Short answer: it's plausible but not ideal.\n\nExplanation and recommendation:\n- loginctl kill-session <id> sends a signal to all processes in the session (can be abrupt).\n- loginctl terminate-session <id> is the cleaner way to end a session (lets logind do the shutdown/cleanup).\n- Many launchers/actions do not run through a shell, so $XDG_SESSION_ID may not be expanded. Wrap the command in a shell if you rely on that env var, or compute the session id explicitly.\n\nSuggested change (use terminate-session and run in a shell):\n````json\n// filepath: layout\n// ...existing code...\n{\n    \"label\" : \"logout\",\n    \"action\" : \"sh -c 'loginctl terminate-session \\\"$XDG_SESSION_ID\\\"'\",\n    \"text\" : \"Logout\",\n    \"keybind\" : \"e\"\n}\n// ...existing code...\n````\n\nGotchas:\n- If $XDG_SESSION_ID is not set in the environment where this action runs, the command will fail. You can either ensure the action runs in your user session environment or resolve the session id dynamically.\n- terminate-session is preferred for a clean logout; kill-session is more forceful.",
